# BRIDGES — Python's vocabulary lifted into the patterns' neutral one, and back down.
#
# `pystrider/intake.py` reflects real Python in ITS OWN names (`for_stmt`, `over`, `binds`, `body`). The
# patterns in `patterns.mf` speak a neutral vocabulary that names no language. Each bridge is the one
# place those two namings are negotiated — which is why adding a second front end costs one bridge, not
# a rewrite of every pattern (O(N) bridges, not O(N²) agreements).
#
# ⚠ A BRIDGE IS A CAST ON THE SOURCE NODE ITSELF. A lift adds the neutral edges to the very node intake
# minted, so `from_code` and `source_line` still hold: what a consumer then recognizes is the ARTIFACT,
# not a copy of it that has lost its provenance.
#
# ⭐⭐ **NO NEUTRAL LABEL APPEARS IN THIS FILE, and that is now structural rather than checked.**
#
# It used to. A lift wrote `LINK F(f) "repeats_over" R(seq)` — restating what `patterns.mf` already
# declared — so the neutral vocabulary lived in two files and could drift: rename a label in one and
# lifted code silently stopped being recognized. We wrote `vocabulary_drift` to catch that, and reported
# the underlying cause as `docs/feedback_microfunctions.md` §6: a bridge could not DELEGATE, because
# `INVOKE` takes a mapping and the `.mf` surface had no way to write one.
#
# ugm shipped named bindings. So a lift now INVOKES the pattern, and a lowering is handed its parts by
# `pystrider.lift.lower`, which gets them from `recognize` — i.e. from the pattern's own description. The
# labels exist in exactly one place, `patterns.mf`, and drift is impossible rather than merely detected.
# Per ugm's own rule: a test guarding a mechanism that exists for lack of the structural answer is a
# smell — the mechanism went, and `vocabulary_drift` went with it.
#
# ⚠ THE PRICE, stated: a delegating bridge is OPAQUE to `establishes` (`unknown = {None}`), because its
# effects happen inside the function it calls. That is the right trade here — static describability was
# only ever wanted in order to police the duplication that is now gone — but it does mean a bridge is an
# ACTION, not a description, and `pystrider.patterns` treats it as one.

# --- LIFTS: Python shape -> neutral description. One parameter; casts the node it is given. -----------

# ⚠ `COPY R(out) F(f)` IS NOT DECORATION — it is how a lift says it cast, and it has to be written by
# hand because `INVOKE` does not do it for us. ugm's own rule is that *a cast returns its subject*, and
# `plan.py` and `execution.py` both implement it (`out.get("result") or args[subject]`) — but `INVOKE`
# writes `out.get("result")` alone, so a stored cast invoked from the ISA answers `None` where the very
# same cast invoked by the planner answers its subject. Two routes, one rule, one of them missing it.
# Reported as `docs/feedback_microfunctions.md` §11; until it moves, every lift says so itself.
fn as_iteration_from_for_stmt(f) -> iteration:
    GET R(seq) F(f) "over"
    GET R(v) F(f) "binds"
    GET R(b) F(f) "body"
    INVOKE R(x) as_iteration it=F(f) seq=R(seq) var=R(v) body=R(b)
    COPY R(out) F(f)

# ⚠⚠ A BRIDGE ABSTAINS WHEN A PART IT NEEDS IS ABSENT, and this one had to learn it the hard way.
#
# `f()` is a call with no `arg` edge — nothing dropped, nothing unreadable, the part is simply not there.
# The bridge used to hand the unset register straight to `as_application`, which `LINK`ed it, and ugm
# minted AN EDGE WHOSE TARGET WAS `None`. So `targets(c, "to")` came back non-empty, every "is this part
# present?" test answered yes, and `f()` was described as **applying `f` to nothing** — a confidently
# wrong reading of code that plainly says otherwise, and the same shape as the placeholder finding in
# `pystrider/intake.py`. We reported the null edge (`docs/feedback_microfunctions.md` §10); ugm now refuses
# the write, so the defect surfaced here as a RuntimeError instead of a lie. ⭐ The refusal is the fix
# working: it converted a silent wrong answer into a loud one, at the instruction that caused it.
#
# ⚠ An ABSENT part is not a GAP. Intake records a gap where it could not read something; here there is
# nothing to read, so the honest answer is that this call is not an application and no cast happens.
# The convention `pystrider.lift` reads: a bridge that cast leaves `out` set; one that abstained does not.
fn as_application_from_call(c) -> application:
    GET R(fn) F(c) "callee"
    GET R(a) F(c) "arg"
    JMPNOT R(a) ".no_argument"
    INVOKE R(x) as_application ap=F(c) func=R(fn) arg=R(a)
    COPY R(out) F(c)
    .no_argument:

# ⚠ Intake calls the test `condition`, NOT `tests` — deliberately, so the two vocabularies cannot agree
# by coincidence. If they shared the word, the bridge would be doing nothing for that part while looking
# like it worked.
fn as_conditional_from_if_stmt(i) -> conditional:
    GET R(t) F(i) "condition"
    GET R(x) F(i) "then"
    GET R(y) F(i) "otherwise"
    INVOKE R(done) as_conditional c=F(i) test=R(t) then_body=R(x) else_body=R(y)
    COPY R(out) F(i)

# --- LOWERINGS: neutral description -> Python shape. -------------------------------------------------
#
# ⚠ A LOWERING IS NOT A LIFT, and the difference is structural rather than nominal: a lift READS what is
# already on a node and casts that same node. A lowering CONSTRUCTS a Python-shaped node that did not
# exist, so it takes the fresh node as its subject plus the parts to hang on it. `lift` applies the
# one-parameter bridges; `lower` applies these. Nothing is keyed on the name.
#
# The parts arrive already extracted — `pystrider.lift.lower` recognizes the description through the
# PATTERN and passes the bindings — so these functions never name a neutral label either. They are pure
# casts in Python's vocabulary, exactly mirroring `patterns.mf`'s casts in the neutral one.

fn as_for_stmt(f, seq, var, body) -> for_stmt:
    LINK F(f) "over" F(seq)
    LINK F(f) "binds" F(var)
    LINK F(f) "body" F(body)

fn as_if_stmt(i, test, then_body, else_body) -> if_stmt:
    LINK F(i) "condition" F(test)
    LINK F(i) "then" F(then_body)
    LINK F(i) "otherwise" F(else_body)

fn as_call(c, func, arg) -> call:
    LINK F(c) "callee" F(func)
    LINK F(c) "arg" F(arg)
